  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 245 AM CDT Sat Sep 5 2026\n\nHot Sunday with scattered showers/storms: A backdoor front will\nenter the region on Sunday. This should provide a stronger \nsource of lift for afternoon and evening pop up showers and \nstorms. There is also a signal for the potential for a few \nstronger pulse thunderstorms as a narrow corridor of 2000 j/kg \nof ML CAPE will exist with higher mid level moisture and higher \ntheta E Diffs. The Ozarks Pulse Thunderstorm Index has the \nhighest signal for storms along and west of Highway 65 and will \nlikely be dependent on the frontal location. Currently precip \nchances are around 20% however if trends continue these may need\nto be increased. Those not in the clouds will experience \nanother scorcher with highs in the upper 90s to low 100s and \nthe Extreme Heat Warning remains in effect for Sunday.\n\nHot Conditions Continue through mid week/Increasing Fire \nDanger: Ensembles continue to suggest that the mid level ridge \nwill remain very close to the area with the center perhaps \nmoving back over the area Tuesday and Wednesday. This would \nallow for the continuation of highs in the upper 90s to lower \n100s however the eastern ozarks could see slightly lower temps \non Monday. Record highs are in the upper 90s for most locations \nTuesday onward and they will be challenged. We will have to make\na determination on the need to extend the Extreme Heat Warning \nfor portions of the area past Sunday. Currently highest \nconfidence to extend would be west of Highway 65. Latest NBM \nguidance is showing slightly higher wind gusts Tuesday and \nWednesday (generally 15-20mph). This could allow for even \nfurther drying out of fuels and increasing fire spread \npotential and we will monitor this closely.\n\nRain Chances Late Week: Ensemble guidance then diverges Thursday\ninto Friday as there is an indication that the ridge may \nretrograde back towards the Rockies which would allow for a \nfront to drop into the area and increase rain chances. While \ncurrently rain chances are in the 20-40% range Thursday and \nFriday, additional updates/changes to these numbers are likely\ngiven this pattern. The NBM temperature data shows larger \nspreads as well which indicates an uncertain late week pattern.",
